  "text": "Solution 1 is the most likely correct answer, though I could not verify it against the literature here.\n\n- **Solution 2** ends with $q^{2D(r-1)}$, which rests on a heuristic count of Borel-type coset representatives. It gives no mechanism that forces that exact exponent, so I don't trust it.\n- **Solution 3** ends with $q^{D(r-1)}$, from the order of the wild kernel $1+\\wp(A/\\wp^r)$. Its unipotence argument is hand-waving. It also assumes the diamond action at $\\wp$ is absorbed into the eigensystem, which is not automatic at level $\\Gamma_1$, and the multiplicity it predicts would blow up with $q$, which is not what a clean control statement looks like.\n- **Solution 1** gives $r$. This matches the form a finite-slope multiplicity result would take in Hattori's work on Drinfeld cuspforms: each finite-slope eigensystem from level $\\mathfrak n\\wp$ reappears at level $\\mathfrak n\\wp^r$ with multiplicity multiplied by $r$. Classically, Hida's control theorem gives constant ordinary rank in $r$, so the Drinfeld answer differs from the classical one, which is worth keeping in mind. I cannot confirm the exact statement without a source, so this rests on the form of the answer rather than a derivation.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
